# CASM WP60 Increment 5 trusted reference: casmopall
#
# One legal statement for each of the 151 documented NMOS 6502/6510
# mnemonic/addressing-mode combinations frozen by the Increment 1 oracle
# (brain/reviews/2026-08-12-casm-phase11-wp60-increment1-opcode-oracle.md),
# in that oracle's own row order. Proves parser, matcher, emitter, PC
# advancement, and file output end to end for every legal combination in one
# native COMP run, complementing test_casm_opcodes' direct in-memory matcher
# coverage of the same 151 tuples (Increment 4).
#
# Hand-derived from the same independently authored oracle values (opcode
# byte + instruction length per tuple), NOT from src/external/casm/opcodes.s
# and NOT by re-deriving NMOS encoding a second time -- Increment 1's
# reconciliation already proved oracle == production byte-for-byte, so this
# reference transcribes the frozen oracle's own opcode/length columns
# mechanically into operand bytes, using the same fixed operand encoding for
# every row:
#   Implied/Accumulator      -> opcode only (1 byte)
#   Immediate/Zero Page(,X/Y)/(Indirect,X)/(Indirect),Y
#                             -> opcode, $12                (2 bytes)
#   Absolute(,X/Y)/Indirect  -> opcode, $34, $12 (little-endian $1234) (3 bytes)
#   Relative                 -> opcode, $00 (branch targets the very next
#                                byte -- see casmopall.seq's TGnn labels)
#
# Source (.ORG $C000) and full per-statement manifest, in emission order.
# Offset columns are 24-bit file byte offsets as native COMP reports them
# (0-based from the start of the raw file stream, which begins with the
# 2-byte PRG load-address header at file offsets $0000-$0001) -- so the
# first mismatching combination can be identified directly from a COMP
# report without reverse-engineering the rest of the file.
#
# | # | Mnemonic | Mode | Offset lo | Offset hi | Bytes |
# | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
